To create a link for every post that is either created or will be created in the future, you can modify the existing `location /` block in your nginx configuration file. You can use the `try_files` directive to try to serve an index.html file for each post, and then use the `return` directive to redirect to the full URL of the post.

Here's an updated configuration that should achieve what you're looking for:
```
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name www.askfundu.com;
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;

    # ... (rest of the configuration remains the same)

    # Create a link for every post
    location /posts {
        try_files $uri $uri/ index.html;
        return 301 $scheme://www.askfundu.com/Posts/$uri;
    }

    # Map post URLs to the correct index.html files
    location ~ ^/posts/([a-zA-Z0-9\-_]+)$ {
        rewrite ^/posts/([a-zA-Z0-9\-_]+)$ /Posts/$1 index.html break;
    }
}
```
This configuration adds two new locations:

1. The `/posts` location tries to serve an index.html file for each post, and then redirects to the full URL of the post using the `return` directive.
2. The `location ~ ^/posts/([a-zA-Z0-9\-_]+)$` location matches URLs that start with `/posts/` followed by a slug (a string of alphanumeric characters, hyphens, and underscores). It uses the `rewrite` directive to rewrite the URL to point to the correct index.html file for the post.

You'll need to create an index.html file for each post in the `/usr/share/nginx/html/Posts` directory, with the same name as the post slug (e.g. `/usr/share/nginx/html/Posts/my-post-name/index.html`).

Make sure to restart your nginx server after updating the configuration file.

